The populist right triumph in Austria, together they make up 29 per cent
The early elections to the Austrian parliament have resulted in the initial results, a noticeable weakening of both parties from today's grand coalition and an exhilarating strengthening of the populist right-wing party.
Strong and special losses have been suffered by the Austrian People's Party (OVP), where the position of the current president, Wilhelm Molterer, is seriously shaking.
On the contrary, the leader of the Alliance for the Future of Austria (BZO), the head of the government of the state of Carintia, J.rg Haider, is preparing his triumphant return to national politics.
According to the approximate results, the social-democracy (SP) remains the strongest party in the country with 29.8 per cent of the vote, losing 5.5 per cent of the vote compared to the last elections in 2006.
Worse is the OVP that loses 25.6 per cent, almost nine per per cent of the vote.
They are the worst, literally cassumphant results in the history of these two major Austrian parties since the post-war period and especially for the people who convened these early elections.
Even at the beginning of July, when the OVP came out of the coalition, the people had a considerable advantage when it came to the SPO.
It is clear, however, that the voters punished them for letting the government shipwreck.
Already in the early reactions to the results there were speculations about the resignation of the president of the party and, until today, vice-chancellor, Wilhelm Molterer.
The observers believe that as early as Monday it could be achieved at the exceptional meeting of the party's presidency.
Such an evolution would evidently facilitate the path of the constant and approximate starting point of the electoral results, i.e. the renewal of the grand coalition between SPO and OVP.
In view of the fact that the reinforcement of both parties of the populist right - the Liberals (FPOs) have won approximately 18 per cent and the BZO, eleven per cent of the vote - at the same time expressed their fears that the same would be repeated in 1999 when the popular negotiated with the populists (then FPO was a unit, but in 2005 it dismembered from BZO) for a coalition government that came to generate a sanction by the European Union.
Haider has clearly bet on this evolution who has already announced that he is prepared to collaborate with any party and who already assumes his return to Vienna, to national politics.
The Green Party has also managed to reach parliament although it has lost slightly and fell from third to fifth place among the Austrian political parties.
Neither other party clearly surpassed the four-per-per-century limit and is therefore not part of parliament.
