Turkey widens its diplomatic horizons
Long before Turkey began to become a member of the European Union, the European powers nested the desire to penetrate the interior.
In the eastern part of the Bosphorus, perhaps just 20 steps into Asia, there is one of the most interesting relics of the disastrous imperial ambition.
The Hydarpasha train station was designed by two German architects, Otto Ritter and Helmut Conu, in neo-Renaissance style.
At the very least, here's what the guides say.
The building's set at the end of the platform and has two towers in front, one for each corner, all looking like a typical German town hall.
The stone's dirty.
Location in your complex is a little decaying.
"But once the vault locker is gone, there's no doubt about what was the company's greatness that this was the westernmost gate.
The station was inaugurated in August 1908 by the German consortium Anatolia-Bagdad Corporation.
Germany, an "latecomer" in the imperial gambling, had the ambition to dominate the Middle East through this railway that was healed to Damascus, Baghdad, and beyond in what is now Saudi Arabia.
However, the ambitions of this imperial Germany failed during the First World War.
Its railway tracks were attacked by Lawrence of Arabia and its irregular Arabs.
Due to the relative isolation of the surrounding regions, generated by Frozen Frozen Cold War borders, it fell low
British dominance and when the forces advanced in Egypt, they pushed the Turks and their German allies out of Palestine and Syria.
The news was too bad for Hyderpasha station.
In 1917 an ammunition train exploded destroying its elaborate roof.
Turkish sources commented that it was sabotage.
Ever since then the station has fallen and stayed out of history.
"But today, this railway, the gateway to Anatolia and all the eastern centers, serves - at least symbolically - to highlight Turkey's willingness to expand its diplomatic horizons.
The Justice-Development and Development Party - the AKP - has planned a new foreign policy for the country.
Relative insolation of the surrounding regions, generated by the Frozen Borders of the Cold War, has disappeared.
Now there's a new policy of collaboration.
The Turkish Government maintains excellent good relations with Israel and Syria, both the Palestinian factions of Hamas and Fatah, with Iraq and Iran and, of course, with the European Union and the United States.
To skip the parameters of this new foreign policy, I climbed into a bunk bed and went to the Turkish capital Ankara, in order to visit one of the leaders of the AKP, an expert on foreign policy, Turkish Prime Minister Suat Kiniklioglu.
The next morning - about ten-and-a-half hours later - after a shaking trip, which produced the cadences of the ancient world in the famous documentary The Night Mail, I arrived in the Turkish capital - a new city, designed to govern a secular new state, far from the Ottoman past.
However, the AKP, with its clear Islamist roots, represents a paradigmatic change in the Turkish consensus.
which has aroused some suspicions in the West and, especially, in Washington.
A taxi takes me to the headquarters of the AKP, an ultra-modern stone-and-steel structure - built ad hoc - in a developing suburb, surrounded by half-built shopping malls and "power cavalries" where middle-class managers in a business gown - men and women - gather early in the morning to agree/make business.
Suat Kiniklioglu could be a centre-right politician of almost every party in Europe.
I brought, tie, I'm sure he'll still have a blackberry.
"The party's foreign policy objective is to re-integrate Turkey into its immediate vicinity.
The Cold War was an an anomaly," he tells me.
Turkey has been severed in relations with its interior.
Today it has to look at the same time to the East and the West.
It's not a case if it isn't towards one or the other.
You've got to do both.
"But surely at some point," I ask, "Should Turkey be forced to choose?"
"Turkey," he replies, "doesn't wanna affront that election."
He then stresses one point that almost everyone I've talked to until that point had recited almost by heart: Turkey's relations not only with the Middle East, but with the Caucasus region, and with the entire Black Sea, represent a boon for Europe and the United States as for Turkey itself.
The message seems to be: with Turkey on board, Europe in its complex can metaphorically mount those railway lines that open the world to eastern Asia and the Middle East.
Again to Hydarpasha station, after a second night without sleep, my thought returns to the two German lords, Herr Ritter and Herr Conu, who had attended the construction of this building, 100 years ago.
How much would they have been surprised to learn that Europe's ambitions in the Middle East, in large part, would be in the hands of Turkey today?
And waiting for the terminal the ferry that will take me back through the Bosphorus, I remember that in those days Turkey was considered "the sick man of Europe."
Then there's not a lot of doubt in what the mainland will be because this fascinating country must claim.
Remembrance Lives: Vernon Handley and William Woodruff
Mark Brown writes: Which Guildford music director since 1962, Vernon "Tod" Handley (esquela, September 11) was responsible for the Guildford Philharmonic Orchestra and the Choir.
As part of the musical project for the city, in the following year it founded its magnificent chorus of young people, Proteus.
It's supposed to be for under-21s, but by the time the Proteus sang its final in 1979, several of us were finishing their 20s - let's just refuse to give even the choir.
The name "Proteus" (the mythological being changing forms) represented the will of the choir and the ability to execute any music that is presented to it, whether madrigal or Messiaen, indifferent if unaccompanied or with complete orchestra.
The musical versatility produced by this chorus of young people was often surprising, always exciting, and it was all due to the inspiration of that man and the excitement for the music that remained absolutely constant throughout the years.
Frequently Tod told us of "leave the music to speak for itself."
He'd say: "The composer knows what he wants.
It's all right there in the music.
That's the way you just have to sing."
His wand was always absolutely clear and precise, though never tax-a-vicious, or too flowery.
Either it's the squeeze, it's always ended up at an even higher level.
John Frayn Turner writes:
"If any driver should have been a gentleman," this is Vernon Handley.
I've known him very well during the two decades he led the Guildford Philharmonic from 1962 to 1982.
Especially over the last 40 years, I've reviewed more than 100 of your concerts and I can honestly say that I've never, for none, been let down - otherwise I've often been inspired by them.
Tod Handley there was that odd combination of greatness and extreme personal modesty.
He hated the drivers who, in his words, simply "sold one or two well-knowledged warhorses around the world."
We'll miss a lot.
- William Woodruff
Professor John Beckett writes:
William Woodruff's sequela (September 25 (September 25) you claim that "in 1946 the studies of economic history were resumed at Oxford and in 1950 he won a Houblon-Norman research scholarship ... at the same time he was studying for a bachelor's degree, a master's degree and a doctoral degree."
It's learned from Woodruff's author's note in Beyond Nab End, but it's not entirely correct.
From 1946 to 1953 he was a professor of economic history at the University of Nottingham, during which he completed a doctoral thesis on the British rubber industry, supervised by Professor J.D. Chambers, and later published in 1958 under the title The British Rubber Industry.
He spent the years between 1950 and 51 in the United States, enjoying a Houblon-Norman scholarship, and the summer of 1952 at the universities of Illinois, Harvard and New York.
In 1953 he definitively left Nottingham for Illinois.
The Reason Because Woodruff opted to miss this part of his career remains unknown.
