Second assault for Honda's best-selling
Honda will start marketing the second-generation jazz in November.
If its presbytress already had a wide habitability, this new monovolume presents an even wider interior space.
To all this are refined mechanics, which increase the comfort of driving and save more fuel.
Evolution in the face of revolution.
That's the Honda Jazz second generation.
Although aesthetical aesthetical the new jazz presents a line very similar to the model that happens, the changes introduced in this monovolume are numerous.
Among others, it is worth mentioning an innovative design, a new platform, larger dimensions and new mechanics.
However, before analyzing the new model it must be noted that this new Jazz arrives six years after introducing the first generation into the market.
Therefore, a first observation is that Honda has renovated this model in a shorter term than that used by other manufacturers.
Although Jazz is less well-known in Europe, in other markets this model is a sales phenomenon.
This is the case of Japan, where Jazz has been the most commercialized car in 2002 and also during 2008.
About one million Jazz units have been sold in Japan since its launch.
The second market has been that of the Old Continent, with about half a million units in six years.
However, Honda intends that this new jazz will increase sales within the segment.
Thus, while in 2007 the Japanese firm marketed a total of 83,000 Jazz units, in 2009 the estimates point to 100,000 cars, which would represent a 17% growth in the European market.
With regard to the Spanish market, Honda has marketed a total of 8,000 Jazz units since 2002, representing 6.7% of Honda's total sales in Spain.
In terms of the new generation, those responsible for the Spanish subsidiary hope to sell 2,000 Jazz units in a full year.
Precisely the arrival of the new Jazz will allow Honda to greatly alleviate the decline in sales in Spain.
In fact, those responsible for the brand hope to end the year with a total of 22,000 units marketed, which represents 4,000 less than in 2007, a 16 per cent decrease.
Despite this increase in Honda sales it has increased its market share in Spain, reaching 1.8%, compared to 1.6% last year.
The Japanese firm also expects to achieve 2 per cent in 2010.
To achieve this quota Honda relies on the arrival of another new model.
It's the Insight, a five-door, compact-size hybrid - 4.37 meters in length - of which the Japanese company expects to market about 200,000 units a year around the world.
By markets and according to the estimates of the Japanese manufacturer, 50% of Insight's sales will occur in the United States, while the remaining percentage will be distributed between the Old Continent and Japan.
The commercial launch of the Insight is planned for the first months of the year, once it is presented in society in the month of January in Valencia.
Regarding the first generation, the new jazz grows at all its heights.
Its length rises to 3.90 meters (five centimeters more), the width to 1.69 meters (two centimeters more) and the distance between axis up to 2.50 meters, representing an increase of five centimeters.
The height, on the other hand, does not change and remains at 1.52 meters.
Along with the increase in dimensions, the inner habitability also grows.
On the one hand to the inclusion of a new platform and, on the one hand, to the introduction of the fuel tank in a central position, a solution also adopted in the previous Jazz.
In addition, the trunk capacity - which has a double bottom system - amounts up to 399 litres, 19 litres more capacity of the trunk of the first generation trunk.
It should be pointed out that these measures are of the version with engine 1.4, since the trunk capacity of Honda Jazz 1.2 is 379 liters.
Other improvements - in addition to outdoor elements such as rear LEDs or the possibility to mount panoramic glass - is an improvement in access to the rear squares.
The gates that give access to the second row have an 80-degree opening angle, compared to the 67-degree of the first jazz.
There are also new engines in this second-generation jazz generation.
At the beginning of its commercialization, this small model will have two gasoline propellants of 1.2 and 1.4 liters.
Both mechanics incorporate the i-VTEC technology that achieves lower consumption, emits less carbon dioxide, achieves excellent mechanical refinement and at the same time achieves a high pair throughout the engine speed.
The first 1,2-litre mechanic gets a power of 90 horses at 6,000 revolutions with a maximum torque of 114 Nm. to 4,900 laps.
This propellant - which in previous Jazz reached 78 horses - has an expenditure of 5.3 liters and emissions of 125 grams of CO2.
Compared to the previous propellant, which in the previous generation achieved 83 horses, it now happens to be 100.
This jazz 1.4 of 100 horsepower achieves a combined consumption of 5.4 litres and 128 grams of CO2.
The 'bee car' that avoids collisions
In order to advance the development of technologies for the prevention of accidents Nissan has been inspired, this time, in nature, in particular bees.
The Japanese company has created a robot that, based on the flight of bees, prevents accidents.
This is the Bio-mimetic Car Robot Drive, or BR23C that is the first step in collision prevention technologies.
The engineers of Nissan Motor Co.'s Advanced Technical Center have developed this small robot in collaboration with the Center for Advanced Science and Technology Research of the prestigious University of Tokyo in Japan.
According to Kazuhiro Doi, Director General of Public Relations for Technology and Research and Brand Management Development, the BR23C is presented as an extension of Nissan Security Shield.
This robot is intended to strengthen the collision prevention capacity of branded vehicles.
During their flight, each bee creates its own oval-shaped personal space that looks a lot like Nissan Security Shield.
But the most important thing are bee-composite eyes, capable of seeing with an angle of more than 300 degrees in all directions, allowing it to fly uninterrupted within its own personal space.
To recreate this visual function, the engineers designed what they have called a laser locator (Laser Range Finder).
It detects obstacles up to two meters away within a 180-degree radius in front of the BR23C, calculates the distance to that object and sends a signal to an on-board microprocessor that immediately results in the prevention of the collision.
"When it detects a hurdle, the tiny robot will mimic the movements of a bee and will immediately change direction by rotating its wheels at a straight or greater angle to prevent the collision.
The biggest difference with any existing system is that the evasion maneuver is totally instinctive.
If that were not the case, the robot wouldn't be able to react quickly enough to avoid obstacles," explains Toshiyuki Andoh, director of the Mobility Laboratory and chief engineer of this project.
Unlike a bee, the robot can't deflect up or down, or diagonally; it can only be moved in two dimensions and in the direction that allows it to rotate its wheels and not in infinite directions and ways like bees.
For this reason, Nissan has used a rotating function, in addition to acceleration and deceleration, so that the small robot avoids collisions.
"This device only needs to process information every few seconds, and act according to that data.
You don't need to steal intuitive information or intuitive logic of previous maneuvers, so it can work continuously with only a couple dozen KB of memory.
Maybe it'll be surprising, but we're not talking about gigas of memory, not even megas.'
It's the first step to developing new instant collision prevention technologies, a technology Nissan predicts will be introduced in serial cars within ten years.
The robot uses lithium-polymer batteries, which are lighter and last longer than the lithium ion variety.
Another possible future application of this technology could be the integration of the unit with a GPS satellite navigation system to guide the driver to their destination by avoiding obstacles along the way.
