Morgan Stanley Sells Spploy to Japanese MUFG
Morgan Stanley announced the entry of Mitsubishi UFJ Financial Group (MUFG), the first Japanese bank, which will disburse nine billion dollars to acquire 21 per cent of its capital, a news that has been accepted with a sharp decline in its stock exchange listing.
A week after undiscovering an interim agreement, the two banks explained that MUFG would buy 9.9 per cent of Morgan Stanley's ordinary shares at the $25.25-a-strong-dollar price.
The agreed price is less than 19 per cent of the book value of the securities as at August 31.
MUFG will invest an additional six billion dollars with the purchase of convertible securities, which will ensure a return of 10 percent and a conversion price of 31.25 dollars.
Morgan Stanley specified that the global operation would be carried out on the basis of a unit price of $29, or 28 per cent above its current change.
The Morgan Stanley stock lost 6.79 per cent to $23.07 each at 17:00 GMT.
"This strategic alliance constitutes an important opportunity to accelerate Morgan Stanley's transition to banking holding status," explained Juan Mack, the CEO of the American group.
Morgan Stanley adopted this new statute last week, which would have to allow him to diversify his activities but he moves under the authority of the Federal Reserve.
His alliance with MUFG also confirms that the current financial crisis forces Wall Street's biggest names to adopt much more cautious behavior than before.
Polls presented last Monday also show that the agreement has been modified over the last few days: Last Monday, Morgan had announced that it would sell as much as 20 per cent of its shares to Japanese for almost $8.5bn.
A week ago, Toshihide Mizuno, the chief executive of MUFG, explained in Tokyo, "we thought the stake would have consisted only of ordinary shares.
But in view of the current situation of the economy and the market, we have wanted to minimize the risks and cover ourselves."
Based on the weighted average price, MUFG will pay its Morgan Stanley securities 7 per cent below its book value.
And the operation will be translated by a dilution of about 20 per cent of profit per share.
The agreement foresees that MUFG will be able to convert within a year to half of its preferred securities into ordinary shares if they are sold at more than 50 per cent above the conversion price, i.e. at $47 or so for a given period.
Morgan Stanley assures that MUFG's capital will allow it to take investment opportunities, strengthen its balance sheet and increase its liquidity.
A spokeswoman for the group, however, refused to give details about the strategic projects of the two new allies.
After MUFG's investment, Morgan Stanley should show a Tier One solvency ratio of about 15 percent, one of the best in the sector.
Morgan said to have greatly eased his balance sheet since the end of August.
In the last few weeks they were gruelling for the group, which saw its stock price fall by half while the cost of security against a risk of defects on its debt took off.
This cost regressed last Monday but remained at a high level of about 12 per cent.
Last week, Goldman Sachs, the only other "survivor" of Wall Street's big investment banks after the bankruptcies of Bear Stearns and Lehman Brothers and the purchase of Merrill Lynch, announced that it had raised $15 billion, in part for Berkshire Hathaway, Warren Buffett's group.
Rafael Correa in Quito on Monday.
Refuse the republic around 21st-century socialism.
This is the common project of the Venezuelan Hugo Chavez, the Bolivian Evo Morales and the Ecuadorian Rafael Correa.
This Sunday, an example of his colleagues and friends, he is the third to vote on a bill for a new Constitution.
Mission fulfilled since 64 per cent of the voters voted "yes" to the referendum, according to the official results referring to 80 per cent of the votes.
According to these results, "no" obtained 28 per cent, 7 per per cent of the votes were null votes and 0.7 per cent were blank votes.
In order for the draft Constitution to be adopted, it had to obtain half the votes plus one.
On Sunday, two polls carried out at the exit of the polling stations and a recount by a non-governmental organization had announced a broad victory to "yes," swindling it between 63 per cent and 70 per cent.
Rafael Correa had also vindicated on the night of Sunday to Monday the victory of his Draft Constitution.
The "Constitution triumphed overwhelmingly ," he had assured, congratulating the Ecuadorians who lead the government of Guayaquil, 280 kilometers south-west of Quito.
Rafael Correa has thus been congratulated by Chavez and Morales but also by his Chilean colleague Micaela Bachelet.
The elections, which will take place in full calm, according to the observer mission of the Organization of American States (OAS), referred to a text of 444 points that consolidated the "new socialism" defended by the president.
The draft Constitution implies an increase in State power over the economy, reserving the "development planning" of a country that lives mainly thanks to the extraction of oil by foreign companies and thanks to foreign exchange sent by their emigrants.
Carried by Correa, elected in 2006, the project also grants strengthened powers to the head of state, who will be able to do up to two consecutive four-year-and-four-year-on-pronged and will be able to dissolve the Assembly.
Correa has already announced that he would use this power and would seek a new mandate in February 2009.
The Constitution also consolidates the principle of free health and educational services that the president began to establish in this country of 13.9 million inhabitants, in which 50 per cent of the population lives below the poverty line.
It finally foresees the interdiction of the presence of foreign troops, which will put an end to the agreement between Quito and Washington for the use of an Ecuadorian base as the starting point for U.S. anti-drug operations.
The 19 people kidnapped have been liberated in Chad
An Egyptian commando threw the assault on the kidnappers of the eleven tourists and their eight companions captured ten days ago.
They're in Cairo.
The eleven European tourists and their eight Egyptian companions kidnapped ten days ago in the desert arrived unharmed in the Egyptian capital after being liberated on Monday afternoon.
By the morning, they had been freed by Egyptian special forces in Chad.
They all looked good on their arrival at 15H30 aboard a special aircraft to the Al Maza military base, adjacent to Cairo International Airport.
Helicopters immediately transported them to Maadi Hospital, a residential suburb of Cairo, to undergo medical tests, so they have not been able to speak to the press.
"They are in good health" and "no ransom has been paid" to the kidnappers, Egyptian Tourism Minister Zoheir Garana said at the airport.
On September 19, these hostages fell to 35 unknown kidnappers while enjoying safari in a desert area at the foot of Mount Ouanat, in the confines of Egypt, Sudan and Libya, and have since moved from one juice to another.
The group consisted of eleven tourists - five Italians, five Germans and one Romanian - and eight Egyptians: two guides, four drivers, a border guard and the director of the Aegyptous travel agency, in charge of the safari organization.
An Egyptian security official said that 30 members of the special forces, transported aboard two helicopters, had launched the assault over the night before dawn.
"An exchange of gunshots took place, killing half of the kidnappers, the others have escaped," said this manager who kept the anonymous.
According to him, he was sent to 150 members of the Egyptian special forces from the "wilight" unit to Sudan, where they were supported by German and Italian commandos.
Italian Foreign Minister Franco Frattini mentioned in Belgrade the role played by secret agents and members of the Italian special forces.
"It must be recognized that we have achieved this result thanks to the professionalism and effectiveness of our men from the secret services, of the special forces," he said without going into details.
In addition, he denied the payment of a ransom.
On the previous day the Sudanese army claimed to have intercepted an SUV during a patrol aboard the kidnarinkers, of which it killed six, including the Chadian boss and arrested two.
He also stated that they were related to a rebel group in Darfur, the Sudan-Unity Liberation Movement (SLA-U), a statement immediately denied by this group.
Letters - Silent Majority of Culture
A debate about the financing of the truffles culture and occupies the current electoral campaign.
That's better.
But an area of our cultural life always remains forgotten.
Who will stand up to denounce the miserable sums given to small museums, archives, archaeological excavations or national historical sites that are starving?
Which politician will dare to remind the federal government to neglect its monuments and heritage buildings?
"Why don't we have any true laws that effectively preserve our patrimony like in Canada and in Quebec?"
Did you know that the French government dedicates three-and-a-half millions of euros to restore only Chinon Castle in the Loire Valley?
In the U.S., it is written in its Constitution that the protection of the heritage is part of the culture of the United States and that this heritage needs to be protected by all the citizens, all the elected and all government departments, and all in consultation.
In Québec, at the Quebec Federation of History Societies, at least 40,000 benevolent people rejoicing in 211 historical, heritage, archives and genealogy societies are worried about searching in the history, keeping piles of private archives with little money and preserving archaeological vestiges.
It's these that are often the watchdogs of our heritage, who protest with energetically and desperation when promoters and managers launch their bulldozers against our old stones and our forgotten cemeteries.
By the time, a memorial for history and patrimony?
The governments of the Netherlands are nationalizing part of the Fortis Bank.
The States of the Netherlands - being the first in Europe - will help with a nationalisation the Belgian-Dutch bank Fortis that is struggling because of the effect of the mortgage crisis of the US market: the three countries will support in total with 11.2bn euros this financial and insurance institution that also has businesses in Hungary.
This weekend the governments of Belgium, Luxembourg and the Netherlands held consultations at a higher level with the President of the European Central Bank and responsible for economic competence of the European Commission.
The Fortis Bank is the first to suffer the crisis in the euro area in the context of the international financial crisis taking place in several waves more than a year ago, therefore the decisions taken with regard to the Bank, in a sense can be regarded as precedent.
According to Sunday's decision, the three states will buy about half (49 per cent) of the bank's property in their area.
The bank will certainly sell a portion of its businesses, including mainly the activities - mainly of insurance - purchased last year by the Dutch ABN Amró.
Fortis shares began a sharp decline last week and in the second half of the week there were already significant signs of crisis in the institution.
Analysts, however, agree to point out that with its 85,000 employees this bank is too big to allow it to break.
The company's shares were down 12 per cent on Friday alone, the lowest point in the past 14 years.
Since January the value of the shares has fallen by more than two-thirds.
Observing current share prices, the group's total capital value on the market is around 14 billion euros, but according to company executives this isn't real.
The bank's management imputed the sharp drop in Fortis's shares to U.S. insecurities over the approval of the $700-billion $US700-billion U.S. package plan.
On Monday the shares already showed a slight increase.
Filip Dierckx was appointed the bank's new CEO on Friday.
The Bank also searched for private investors, but so far no one has given a specific offer.
The Belgian government had previously assured that investors didn't have to worry, they won't lose their money.
Among the bank's clients so far there was no panic, they didn't invade the banking offices to withdraw their money.
The Fortis has its own branch in Hungary and has also bought leasing companies.
Zorro in the henhouse
In the U.S., life-and-health insurance companies by looking at drug purchase data often refuse to sign an insurance contract.
Privacy policy pundits fear that the personal data market will become uncontrollable.
"There are personal data protection programmes, which in the use of recipe data in all cases is linked to the approval of the given person.
It must be in independent hands, because if we don't, "confy the hen to the fox" - the Washington Post quote Richard Dick, designer of the medical prescription follow-up program.
Insured companies make the price of the insurance and the amount of compensating of the customer's state of health and if they believe that the risk is excessive, they simply don't make the contract.
Earlier they shipped the client to a medical control and signed the contract with him knowing the outcome of the control.
Dick ten years ago invented a much cheaper procedure: with the customer's identification number he easily gets the list of medications purchased in the last year and a half and the program itself draws the conclusion about the customer's diseases and state of health.
For example, by systematically taking large doses of cholesterol-lowering pills, it indicates that the level of fat in the blood is almost incurably high, thus being immediately included in a higher-than-at-at-at-risk category.
Numerous versions of the program circulate, some even establish a number of risk, so the insurance company in addition to the administration has little to do, although there are small difficulties in this regard.
Disadvantages of the program include that some medicines are multi-purpose, for example certain substances serve both to treat headache and heatwaves and even anti-depressants.
The latter is an important risk-imbursing factor for non-insurance companies while the former aren't.
Special companies have been set up to consult and process this data, which before purchasing the necessary information from the pharmacies, request the patient's approval.
The authorities have already forced them into this, although they did not receive a fine for the negligence until now.
Lately, some of the companies have also developed the results of lab exams and linking them with drug use data they obtain more accurate information about the patient's state of health.
More and more companies are gaining access to this data and it's becoming increasingly difficult to control its use.
There is an exclusive law for the protection of personal health data, but it is applied only to insurance companies and those providing health services.
There is already before Congress the bill that provides the right of the authority for the control of specialized companies for the provision of personal data.
Dick Richard admits that this is insufficient: therefore he developed his program, which will require the specific approval of the person for the provision of his own personal data.
It's another issue that such a person couldn't deny his approval, because otherwise the insurance companies won't sign the contract with him.
So the hens, in spite of everything, are exposed to the fox's goodwill.
Marseille Global Menu
The European Union Douizes Its Trade with India
As the European Union and India are mutually trading partners, this issue in addition to the other mostly global problems dominated the agenda for the ninth summit.
There will now also be a Franco-Indian summit to deal mainly with cooperation in nuclear energy.
Although the European Union recognizes that India is a great democratic power in development that should have a greater voice and vote in international affairs but the member countries have not yet been able to reach a common denominator on whether they should support New Delhi as a permanent member of the U.N. Security Council.
This issue has not been dealt with at the ninth summit in Marseille yesterday.
The French President Nicolas Sarkozy, current president of the EU, José Manuel Barroso President of the Commission and Javier Solana as the main representative of the foreign policy, received Manmohan Singh, Prime Minister of India with whom they reached an agreement to double in five years, i.e. an increase of more than 100 billion euros, their commercial exchange.
Given that between 2000 and 2007, merchandise trading increased from $25bn to $55 billion, this is not an excessively ambitious target.
Thus they legally harmonized the twenty-six existing bilateral civil aviation agreements with India, so air transport will also be able to fly in the future.
By the end of 2009, India wants to sign the free-to-trade agreement with the Union.
Sarkozy has vowed to accelerate negotiations.
The objective is the balance, because while the EU is India &apos; s first trading partner, New Delhi only ranks ninth among the Union's trading partners.
The summit turned its attention to global issues.
Both sides condemned the Afghan and Pakistani situation and terrorism - all forms of the latter - and pressed the convening of a U.N. conference.
The conclusion of the Doha round of trade liberalization was also welcomed, although both sides have a great responsibility for their failure.
The EU confirmed that it will cling to its energy and climate program, i.e. until 2020 it wants to reduce polluting emissions by at least 20 percent.
India also agrees with this, just as at next year's climate conference a global agreement should be reached and the production of greenhouse gases reduced by 50 per per cent by 50 per per cent.
They also wanted to hinder the rise in food prices and supported the convening of an international conference to review the financial crisis.
India's head of government will travel to the French capital today, where - at a bilateral meeting - he will sign an agreement on the peaceful use of nuclear energy.
Paris is confident that the French companies will receive assignments worth 20 billion euros to build nuclear reactors.
According to news agency information, the agreement would be for a period of 15 years.
In Frankfurt more than 300 Italian publishers
Strong presence of Italian publishers at the international exhibition of the Frankfurt book.
There will be more than three hundred Italian publishers present to the 60th edition of the Buchmesse, the most important international meeting for the exchange of rights.
The International Exhibition of the Frankfurt book awaits for this edition more than 7 thousand exhibitors from 102 different countries that will occupy the 171,790 square meters of the exhibition with more than 403 thousand titles on display and with Turkey as a guest of honor.
It will be the undersecretary of the Ministry of Cultural Property and Activities Francisco Maria Giro to represent the Italian government at the inauguration ceremony of the Italian pavilion: Italy will institutionally and effectively be present with a point Italy - 264 square meters of exhibition space.
There will be an encounter with the print at the Room Struktur in pavilion 5.0 of the Buchmesse: this will be the opportunity to present numbers, trends and forward-to-does about the status of publishers in Italy.
According to the data, the weight of the books of foreign authors gradually decreases over the total of Italian production, and in particular that for adults and adolescents for adults and adolescents.
Found Steve Fossett's jet the missing millionaire a year ago
The wreckage of Steve Fossett's plane, the millionaire who vanished on September 3, 2007 while flying over the Nevada Desert, has been found.
The rubble has been stunned on a 3,000-foo-footer in the Nevada mountain area of Mammoth Lakes, on the border between California and Nevada.
There was no human wreckage inside the plane.
Wood County Sheriff's commissioner, John Anderson, has dissipated the doubts that remained: "The plane has been located, we've sent a squad, and now we've got the confirmation that it's typical of what Steve Fossett was flying on the day of his demise," he said at a print conference.
The commissioner has specified that no human wreckage has been found either on the plane or in the surrounding area.
The billionaire, 63, has been pronounced dead on Feb.
The U.S. Office for Transport Safety(Ntsb has made it known that a few hours after the sighting of the wreckage has been sent a squadron of investigators who has identified the device: it is Bellanca 8kcab, dispersed since September 3, 2007, when Fossett took off from Yerington in Nevada for a three-hour flight.
Yesterday, always in California, not far from Nevada's confidance, some Fossett personal remains have been found by a couple near their Mammoth Lakes home.
These are two documents called Fossett, the two issued by the Illinois Federal Aviation Administration, cash and a sweatshirt.
Search for Fossett had been disrupted a month after his disappearance.
The millionaire had conquered a place in history thanks to a series of scops.
In 2002, at the sixth, he had become the first man to complete the world's spin on a hot air balloon: two lone-flying weeks.
Thirty-three years later he had turned the world by airplane and non-stop.
Among his records, also for having flown to 15,445 meters of height, over the Andes, but with a co-pilot.
